 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "The term spread is the 10-year Government of India par yield minus the 91-day Treasury bill primary yield, computed here from two columns of the same RBI weekly 'Ratios and Rates' table so both legs share one vintage. It is derived by this dashboard and is not published as a series by RBI. A positive, normally-sloped curve is the usual state and reflects term and liquidity premia; a flattening or inverted curve (spread at or below zero) signals tight monetary conditions or expected slowdown; a very steep curve reflects easing expectations, heavy long-end government supply or inflation risk. It is used here as a compact read on the shape of the yield curve.",
   "source_name": "Derived in this dashboard from RBI WSS 'Ratios and Rates' — 10-Year G-Sec Par Yield (FBIL) and 91-Day Treasury Bill Primary Yield; the ratio/spread is not an RBI series",
